  "summary": "Aneurin “Nye” Bevan (1897–1960) was a Welsh Labour politician and a leading figure in the creation of Britain’s welfare state. The son of a coal miner, he left school at 14, worked in the mines and became active in the South Wales miners’ union. After helping lead miners during the 1926 General Strike, he entered Parliament in 1929 as MP for Ebbw Vale.\n\nKnown for his powerful speeches and sharp criticism of political opponents—including Winston Churchill—Bevan was a prominent voice on Labour’s left. In Clement Attlee’s postwar government, he became Minister of Health and led the campaign to establish the National Health Service. Inspired by a medical scheme in his hometown of Tredegar, he championed healthcare free at the point of use. The NHS Act passed in 1946, and the service began in 1948, bringing more than 2,500 hospitals into public ownership.\n\nBevan resigned from the government in 1951 over proposed charges for some dental and vision services and the use of National Insurance funds for rearmament. His supporters became known as “Bevanites.” He later held senior positions in Labour’s opposition, including Deputy Leader, and died of stomach cancer in 1960, aged 62. He is widely remembered as one of Britain’s most influential left-wing politicians and was voted first in a 2004 list of 100 Welsh Heroes.",
